= Semantic network =

== Overview ==
A '''semantic network''' is a graph representation in which nodes denote concepts, entities or propositions and labeled links denote semantic relations among them.

Semantic networks have been used in:
* artificial intelligence;
* cognitive science;
* linguistics;
* knowledge organization;
* education;
* expert systems.

They are historically important predecessors and relatives of modern [[Knowledge graph|knowledge graphs]] and frame-based representations.

== Basic structure ==
A simple semantic network might contain:

<pre>
Canary ──isA────> Bird
Bird ───hasPart─> Wing
Bird ───can─────> Fly
</pre>

The graph structure makes relationships visible.

Depending on the system, links can support inheritance or activation.

== Historical development ==
M. Ross Quillian's work on semantic memory in the 1960s modeled word meanings and concepts as nodes connected through semantic relationships.

Later psychological work by Collins and Quillian, and Collins and Loftus, used networks to model semantic memory, category structure and spreading activation.

AI research also developed semantic nets as symbolic knowledge representations.

== Inheritance ==
Some semantic networks use hierarchical inheritance.

If:

<pre>
Canary isA Bird
Bird hasProperty canFly
</pre>

a system may infer:

<pre>
Canary canFly
</pre>

But exceptions such as penguins show that default inheritance is not the same as strict logical entailment.

Different semantic-network systems handled inheritance differently.

== Spreading activation ==
Cognitive semantic-network models often use '''spreading activation'''.

Activating one concept increases activation of nearby concepts through weighted links.

This can model:
* priming;
* associative retrieval;
* semantic distance.

Spreading activation is a cognitive/computational mechanism, not a logical proof procedure.

== Semantic network and ontology ==
A semantic network can represent concepts and relations without supplying a fully formal [[Ontology|ontology]].

Early semantic nets often used link labels whose exact logic was implicit or system-specific.

Formal ontology languages later emphasized explicit model-theoretic semantics and carefully defined relation properties.

== Semantic network and knowledge graph ==
Modern [[Knowledge graph|knowledge graphs]] share graph-based representation.

The distinction is historical and practical rather than absolute.

Knowledge graphs often add:
* globally scoped identifiers;
* schema/ontology;
* provenance;
* large-scale data integration;
* graph queries;
* deductive or learned completion.

Semantic networks often emphasize conceptual/cognitive links and older KR traditions.

== Semantic network and Semantic Web ==
A semantic network is not the [[Semantic Web]].

The Semantic Web is a standards-based Web architecture using technologies such as RDF, OWL and SPARQL.

A semantic network is a more general representation pattern.

It can exist entirely outside Web standards.

== Link semantics ==
Semantic networks can use relations such as:
* is-a;
* part-of;
* causes;
* associated-with;
* has-property;
* instance-of.

The semantic force of these relations depends on definitions.

A graph with visually labeled links does not automatically specify which inferences are valid.

== Frames ==
Frame systems developed alongside semantic networks.

A frame represents a concept or situation using named slots and values.

Networks and frames can be intertranslated in many cases, but frames emphasize structured records while networks emphasize relational topology.

== Epistemic notes ==
Semantic networks are useful for representing association and conceptual structure, but the word "semantic" does not guarantee formal semantics. The inference behavior must be specified separately.

